Q: Does JavaScript provide a built-in way to detect stale async results? A: <p>I've tried and looked at some other methods. As some commenters have said, JavaScript does not provide a built-in way to detect or automatically ignore stale async results. It has to be handled explicitly in the code since “stale” is application-defined, not a language concept. For anyone who wants to see the different ways people go about implementing it, I'll share some here:</p>
<p>An alternative approach is to use <code>AbortController</code> to cancel the request itself, but it doesn't work everywhere, only for <code>fetch</code>:</p>
<pre><code>let controller;

async function search(query) {
  if (controller) controller.abort();

  controller = new AbortController();

  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api?q=${query}`, {
      signal: controller.signal
    });
    const data = await res.json();
    display(data);
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.name === 'AbortError') return;
    throw e;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The method used in the question is a standard, but the cleaner approach is to use a request id:</p>
<pre><code>let requestId = 0;

async function search(query) {
  const id = ++requestId;

  const res = await fetch(`/api?q=${query}`);
  const data = await res.json();

  if (id !== requestId) return;

  display(data);
} 
</code></pre>
<p>Another approach is deduplication, expecially for caching layers and APIs.  It doesn't actually solve the stale issue, but it can prevent duplicate requests, and when combined with the cleaner approach above, it can prevent both duplicates and stale results:</p>
<pre><code>let version = 0;
const inFlight = new Map();

async function search(query) {
  const v = ++version;

  if (inFlight.has(query)) {
    const data = await inFlight.get(query);
    if (v !== version) return;
    display(data);
    return;
  }

  const promise = fetch(`/api?q=${query}`)
    .then(res =&gt; res.json())
    .finally(() =&gt; inFlight.delete(query));

  inFlight.set(query, promise);

  const data = await promise;

  if (v !== version) return;

  display(data);
}
</code></pre>
